window.docContents[189].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras--Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.14. Tensor products and duals of representations of Lie algebras","header":"Tensor product of Lie algebra representations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras--Tensor-product-of-Lie-algebra-representations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Section-4___2___-Characters/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.2. Characters\u0009Section 4.2: Characters\u0009Section 4.2: Characters","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Section-4___2___-Characters/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___2___formalization"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___7___-Orthogonality-of-matrix-elements/Orthogonality-of-matrix-elements-of-irreducible-representations/#Chapter4___Proposition4___7___1":{"contents":"Proposition 4.7.1. (i) Matrix elements of nonisomorphic irreducible representations are orthogonal in F(G, \\mathbb{C}) under the form (f, g) = \\frac{1}{|G|} \\sum_{x \\in G} f(x)\\overline{g(x)}.\n\n(ii) One has (t^V_{ij}, t^V_{i'j'}) = \\delta_{ii'}\\delta_{jj'} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\dim V}.\n\nThus, matrix elements of irreducible representations of G form an orthogonal basis of F(G, \\mathbb{C}).\n\nProof. Let V and W be two irreducible representations of G. Take \\{v_i\\} to be an orthonormal basis of V and \\{w_i\\} to be an orthonormal basis of W under their positive definite invariant Hermitian forms. Let w^*_i \\in W^* be the linear function on W defined by taking the inner product with w_i: w^*_i(u) = (u, w_i). Then for x \\in G we have\n(xw^*_i, w^*_j) = \\overline{(xw_i, w_j)}. Therefore, putting P = \\frac{1}{|G|} \\sum_{x \\in G} x, we have\n\n(t^V_{ij}, t^W_{i'j'}) = |G|^{-1} \\sum_{x \\in G} (xv_i, v_j)\\overline{(xw_{i'}, w_{j'})}\n\n= |G|^{-1} \\sum_{x \\in G} (xv_i, v_j)(xw^*_{i'}, w^*_{j'}) = (P(v_i \\otimes w^*_{i'}), v_j \\otimes w^*_{j'}).\n\nIf V \\neq W, this is zero, since P projects to the trivial representation, which does not occur in V \\otimes W^*. If V = W, we need to consider (P(v_i \\otimes v^*_{i'}), v_j \\otimes v^*_{j'}). We have a G-invariant decomposition\n\nV \\otimes V^* = \\mathbb{C} \\oplus L,\n\n\\mathbb{C} = \\operatorname{span}(\\sum v_k \\otimes v^*_k),\n\nL = \\operatorname{span}_{a: \\sum_k a_{kk} = 0}(\\sum_{k,l} a_{kl} v_k \\otimes v^*_l),\n\nand P projects to the first summand along the second one. The projection of v_i \\otimes v^*_{i'} to \\mathbb{C} \\subset \\mathbb{C} \\oplus L is thus\n\n\\frac{\\delta_{ii'}}{\\dim V} \\sum v_k \\otimes v^*_k.\n\nThis shows that\n\n(P(v_i \\otimes v^*_{i'}), v_j \\otimes v^*_{j'}) = \\frac{\\delta_{ii'}\\delta_{jj'}}{\\dim V},\n\nwhich finishes the proof of (i) and (ii). The last statement follows immediately from the sum of squares formula. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.7. Orthogonality of matrix elements\u0009Orthogonality of matrix elements of irreducible representations","header":"Orthogonality of matrix elements of irreducible representations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___7___-Orthogonality-of-matrix-elements/Orthogonality-of-matrix-elements-of-irreducible-representations/#Chapter4___Proposition4___7___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Find-the-sum-of-dimensions-of-all-irreducible-representations-of-S_n/#Chapter5___Problem5___12___5___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.12. Representations of $S_n$\u0009Find the sum of dimensions of all irreducible representations of S_n\u0009Find the sum of dimensions of all irreducible representations of S_n","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Find-the-sum-of-dimensions-of-all-irreducible-representations-of-S_n/#Chapter5___Problem5___12___5___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Schur-Weyl-duality___-V_________n___-___-___-V_lambda-___-L_lambda/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____--Schur-Weyl-duality___-V_________n___-___-___-V_lambda-___-L_lambda":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.18. Schur-Weyl duality for $\\mathfrak{gl}(V)$","header":"Schur-Weyl duality: V^{⊗n} = ⊕ V_lambda ⊗ L_lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Schur-Weyl-duality___-V_________n___-___-___-V_lambda-___-L_lambda/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____--Schur-Weyl-duality___-V_________n___-___-___-V_lambda-___-L_lambda"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Equivalence-of-Definitions-5___2___1-and-5___2___2/#Chapter5___Proposition5___2___3":{"contents":"Proposition 5.2.3. Definitions (5.2.1) and (5.2.2) are equivalent.\n\nProof. To show that the condition of Definition 5.2.2 implies the condition of Definition 5.2.1, notice that z is a root of the characteristic polynomial of the matrix (a monic polynomial with rational, respectively integer, coefficients). To establish the converse, suppose z is a root of\n\np(x) = x^n + a_1 x^{n-1} + \\ldots + a_{n-1} x + a_n.\n\nThen the characteristic polynomial of the following matrix (called the companion matrix) is p(x):\n\n\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 & \\ldots & 0 & -a_n \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & \\ldots & 0 & -a_{n-1} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\ldots & 0 & -a_{n-2} \\\\ & & & \\vdots & & \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\ldots & 1 & -a_1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\nSince z is a root of the characteristic polynomial of this matrix, it is an eigenvalue of this matrix. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.2. Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers\u0009Equivalence of Definitions 5.2.1 and 5.2.2","header":"Equivalence of Definitions 5.2.1 and 5.2.2","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Equivalence-of-Definitions-5___2___1-and-5___2___2/#Chapter5___Proposition5___2___3"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Existence-of-V-in-N-with-nonzero-character-value/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Existence-of-V-in-N-with-nonzero-character-value--Existence-of-V-in-N-with-nonzero-character-value--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"A finite group element with conjugacy class of positive prime-power size admits a\nnontrivial simple complex representation whose dimension is not divisible by that prime and\nwhose character does not vanish at the element.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.6; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Lemma5.4.7; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Existence of V in N with nonzero character value\u0009Existence of V in N with nonzero character value\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Existence-of-V-in-N-with-nonzero-character-value/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Existence-of-V-in-N-with-nonzero-character-value--Existence-of-V-in-N-with-nonzero-character-value--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Category/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category--Category--Category--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A universe-polymorphic operation assigning to each type a type in a universe large enough for the input level and an additional successor level.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Definition7.1.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.1. The definition of a category\u0009Category\u0009Category\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Category/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category--Category--Category--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});